INSERT INTO `stage_games` (`id`, `stage_id`, `game_mode_id`, `game_order`, `title`, `description`, `instructions`, `xp_reward`, `coin_reward`, `game_data`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(1, 1, 1, 1, 'Puzzle — The Life of Hunter-Gatherers', 'Puzzle game for Stage 1: The Life of Hunter-Gatherers', 'Play the Puzzle activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 75, 15, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 1, \"title\": \"The Life of Hunter-Gatherers\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\nWho were the earliest people?\\nPeople lived in the Indian subcontinent as early as two million years ago. We describe these early people as hunter-gatherers because of the way they obtained their food.\\nThey generally obtained food by:\\n🏹 Hunting wild animals\\n🐟 Catching fish\\n🐦 Catching birds\\n🍎 Gathering fruits\\n🌱 Gathering roots\\n🌰 Gathering nuts\\n🌾 Gathering seeds\\n🍃 Gathering leaves and stalks\\n🥚 Collecting eggs\\nGathering plant produce was especially important because the subcontinent had a large variety of plants.\\n🌿 Knowledge Needed for Survival\\nLife was not easy for hunter-gatherers.\\nTo hunt animals or catch fish and birds, people needed to be:\\nAlert\\nQuick\\nAble to react intelligently\\nTo gather plants, they needed to know:\\nWhich plants were edible\\nWhich plants or parts of plants could be poisonous\\nWhich season different fruits became ripe\\nThis means hunter-gatherers needed considerable knowledge of their environment to survive.\\n🚶 Why Did Hunter-Gatherers Move?\\nHunter-gatherers did not normally stay permanently in one place.\\nThere were several reasons for their movement.\\n1. Food resources could run out\\nIf people stayed in one place for a long time, they could use up the available plant and animal resources.\\nSo they had to move somewhere else in search of food.\\n2. Animals moved\\nAnimals themselves moved in search of food.\\nFor example, deer and wild cattle moved in search of grass and leaves.\\nTherefore, hunters had to follow the movement of animals.\\n3. Plants were seasonal\\nDifferent plants produced fruits in different seasons.\\nPeople therefore moved from place to place depending on the availability of different plants.\\n4. Water was necessary\\nPeople, plants and animals all needed water.\\nWater could be found in:\\nLakes\\nStreams\\nRivers\\nSome water sources were seasonal, so people sometimes had to move in search of water during dry periods.\\n5. Meeting people\\nPeople may also have travelled to meet their friends and relatives.\\nImportantly, they travelled on foot.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Who Were Hunter-Gatherers?\\nQuestion:\\nWhy are early people described as hunter-gatherers?\\nA. They lived only by farming crops\\nB. They hunted animals and gathered food from nature\\nC. They lived permanently in large cities\\nD. They traded food with other communities\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nThey hunted wild animals, caught fish and birds, and gathered fruits, roots, nuts, seeds, leaves, stalks and eggs.\\nMCQ 2 — Why Did They Move?\\nQuestion:\\nA group has stayed in one place for a long time. Most of the available food resources are now used up. What would the group most likely do?\\nA. Stay there permanently\\nB. Stop eating food\\nC. Move to another place in search of food\\nD. Build a modern farm\\n✅ Correct Answer: C\\nExplanation:\\nHunter-gatherers moved when available plant and animal resources became limited. Animals also moved, plants produced fruit in different seasons, and water could become unavailable.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Should We Stay or Move?\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player reaches a hunter-gatherer camp.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Look around carefully. The group has been here for some time. Something has changed. Should they stay or move?”\\nThe player must inspect the environment and identify the clues.\\n🔎 Clues\\nThe player can interact with:\\n🍂 Empty food-gathering area\\n“Most nearby plant food has already been collected.”\\n🐾 Animal tracks\\n“The animals the group follows have moved away.”\\n🌱 Seasonal plants\\n“Some plants provide food during different seasons.”\\n💧 Dry water source\\n“This water source is no longer providing enough water.”\\n🏠 Comfortable shelter\\n“The shelter is still usable.”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nSelect all the clues that could explain why the group should move.\\nCorrect selections:\\n✅ 🍂 Food resources becoming limited\\n✅ 🐾 Animals moving away\\n✅ 🌱 Seasonal availability of plants\\n✅ 💧 Water becoming unavailable\\n❌ 🏠 Shelter is still usable\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Hunter-gatherers depended on nature for food and water. When resources changed or became limited, they moved to another place.”\\nThis works well as a puzzle because the player has to apply the information to an environment, rather than answer a direct question.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Empty Camp\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an old hunter-gatherer campsite.\\nThere is no one there.\\nAI Guide:\\n“Something happened here. The camp is empty, but there are clues all around you. Investigate them and find out why the group left.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nThe player must discover four clues.\\nClue 1 — 🍂 Food Area\\nThe player examines the nearby plants.\\nEvidence: Very little edible plant food remains.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The group may have used up much of the available plant food.”\\nClue 2 — 🐾 Animal Tracks\\nThe player follows animal tracks.\\nEvidence: The animals have moved away from the area.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The animals they hunted did not remain in one place.”\\nClue 3 — 💧 Water Source\\nThe player investigates the nearby water source.\\nEvidence: The water source is seasonal and currently has little/no water.\\nAI Guide:\\n“Water availability could also have forced the group to move.”\\nClue 4 — 🌿 Seasonal Plants\\nThe player examines plants from another area.\\nEvidence: Different plants provide food in different seasons.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The availability of plant foods changed with the seasons.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhy did the group leave?\\nA. They wanted to build a city\\nB. Their food, animals and water resources were changing or becoming limited\\nC. They had discovered machines\\nD. They wanted to travel by train\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The group depended on nature for survival. As food, animals, plants and water changed or became limited, they moved to another place.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:55', '2026-09-10 04:34:55'),
(2, 1, 2, 2, 'Mystery — The Life of Hunter-Gatherers', 'Mystery game for Stage 1: The Life of Hunter-Gatherers', 'Play the Mystery activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 100, 20, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 1, \"title\": \"The Life of Hunter-Gatherers\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\nWho were the earliest people?\\nPeople lived in the Indian subcontinent as early as two million years ago. We describe these early people as hunter-gatherers because of the way they obtained their food.\\nThey generally obtained food by:\\n🏹 Hunting wild animals\\n🐟 Catching fish\\n🐦 Catching birds\\n🍎 Gathering fruits\\n🌱 Gathering roots\\n🌰 Gathering nuts\\n🌾 Gathering seeds\\n🍃 Gathering leaves and stalks\\n🥚 Collecting eggs\\nGathering plant produce was especially important because the subcontinent had a large variety of plants.\\n🌿 Knowledge Needed for Survival\\nLife was not easy for hunter-gatherers.\\nTo hunt animals or catch fish and birds, people needed to be:\\nAlert\\nQuick\\nAble to react intelligently\\nTo gather plants, they needed to know:\\nWhich plants were edible\\nWhich plants or parts of plants could be poisonous\\nWhich season different fruits became ripe\\nThis means hunter-gatherers needed considerable knowledge of their environment to survive.\\n🚶 Why Did Hunter-Gatherers Move?\\nHunter-gatherers did not normally stay permanently in one place.\\nThere were several reasons for their movement.\\n1. Food resources could run out\\nIf people stayed in one place for a long time, they could use up the available plant and animal resources.\\nSo they had to move somewhere else in search of food.\\n2. Animals moved\\nAnimals themselves moved in search of food.\\nFor example, deer and wild cattle moved in search of grass and leaves.\\nTherefore, hunters had to follow the movement of animals.\\n3. Plants were seasonal\\nDifferent plants produced fruits in different seasons.\\nPeople therefore moved from place to place depending on the availability of different plants.\\n4. Water was necessary\\nPeople, plants and animals all needed water.\\nWater could be found in:\\nLakes\\nStreams\\nRivers\\nSome water sources were seasonal, so people sometimes had to move in search of water during dry periods.\\n5. Meeting people\\nPeople may also have travelled to meet their friends and relatives.\\nImportantly, they travelled on foot.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Who Were Hunter-Gatherers?\\nQuestion:\\nWhy are early people described as hunter-gatherers?\\nA. They lived only by farming crops\\nB. They hunted animals and gathered food from nature\\nC. They lived permanently in large cities\\nD. They traded food with other communities\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nThey hunted wild animals, caught fish and birds, and gathered fruits, roots, nuts, seeds, leaves, stalks and eggs.\\nMCQ 2 — Why Did They Move?\\nQuestion:\\nA group has stayed in one place for a long time. Most of the available food resources are now used up. What would the group most likely do?\\nA. Stay there permanently\\nB. Stop eating food\\nC. Move to another place in search of food\\nD. Build a modern farm\\n✅ Correct Answer: C\\nExplanation:\\nHunter-gatherers moved when available plant and animal resources became limited. Animals also moved, plants produced fruit in different seasons, and water could become unavailable.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Should We Stay or Move?\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player reaches a hunter-gatherer camp.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Look around carefully. The group has been here for some time. Something has changed. Should they stay or move?”\\nThe player must inspect the environment and identify the clues.\\n🔎 Clues\\nThe player can interact with:\\n🍂 Empty food-gathering area\\n“Most nearby plant food has already been collected.”\\n🐾 Animal tracks\\n“The animals the group follows have moved away.”\\n🌱 Seasonal plants\\n“Some plants provide food during different seasons.”\\n💧 Dry water source\\n“This water source is no longer providing enough water.”\\n🏠 Comfortable shelter\\n“The shelter is still usable.”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nSelect all the clues that could explain why the group should move.\\nCorrect selections:\\n✅ 🍂 Food resources becoming limited\\n✅ 🐾 Animals moving away\\n✅ 🌱 Seasonal availability of plants\\n✅ 💧 Water becoming unavailable\\n❌ 🏠 Shelter is still usable\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Hunter-gatherers depended on nature for food and water. When resources changed or became limited, they moved to another place.”\\nThis works well as a puzzle because the player has to apply the information to an environment, rather than answer a direct question.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Empty Camp\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an old hunter-gatherer campsite.\\nThere is no one there.\\nAI Guide:\\n“Something happened here. The camp is empty, but there are clues all around you. Investigate them and find out why the group left.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nThe player must discover four clues.\\nClue 1 — 🍂 Food Area\\nThe player examines the nearby plants.\\nEvidence: Very little edible plant food remains.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The group may have used up much of the available plant food.”\\nClue 2 — 🐾 Animal Tracks\\nThe player follows animal tracks.\\nEvidence: The animals have moved away from the area.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The animals they hunted did not remain in one place.”\\nClue 3 — 💧 Water Source\\nThe player investigates the nearby water source.\\nEvidence: The water source is seasonal and currently has little/no water.\\nAI Guide:\\n“Water availability could also have forced the group to move.”\\nClue 4 — 🌿 Seasonal Plants\\nThe player examines plants from another area.\\nEvidence: Different plants provide food in different seasons.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The availability of plant foods changed with the seasons.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhy did the group leave?\\nA. They wanted to build a city\\nB. Their food, animals and water resources were changing or becoming limited\\nC. They had discovered machines\\nD. They wanted to travel by train\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The group depended on nature for survival. As food, animals, plants and water changed or became limited, they moved to another place.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
(3, 1, 3, 3, 'Theoretical — The Life of Hunter-Gatherers', 'Theoretical game for Stage 1: The Life of Hunter-Gatherers', 'Play the Theoretical activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 50, 10, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 1, \"title\": \"The Life of Hunter-Gatherers\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\nWho were the earliest people?\\nPeople lived in the Indian subcontinent as early as two million years ago. We describe these early people as hunter-gatherers because of the way they obtained their food.\\nThey generally obtained food by:\\n🏹 Hunting wild animals\\n🐟 Catching fish\\n🐦 Catching birds\\n🍎 Gathering fruits\\n🌱 Gathering roots\\n🌰 Gathering nuts\\n🌾 Gathering seeds\\n🍃 Gathering leaves and stalks\\n🥚 Collecting eggs\\nGathering plant produce was especially important because the subcontinent had a large variety of plants.\\n🌿 Knowledge Needed for Survival\\nLife was not easy for hunter-gatherers.\\nTo hunt animals or catch fish and birds, people needed to be:\\nAlert\\nQuick\\nAble to react intelligently\\nTo gather plants, they needed to know:\\nWhich plants were edible\\nWhich plants or parts of plants could be poisonous\\nWhich season different fruits became ripe\\nThis means hunter-gatherers needed considerable knowledge of their environment to survive.\\n🚶 Why Did Hunter-Gatherers Move?\\nHunter-gatherers did not normally stay permanently in one place.\\nThere were several reasons for their movement.\\n1. Food resources could run out\\nIf people stayed in one place for a long time, they could use up the available plant and animal resources.\\nSo they had to move somewhere else in search of food.\\n2. Animals moved\\nAnimals themselves moved in search of food.\\nFor example, deer and wild cattle moved in search of grass and leaves.\\nTherefore, hunters had to follow the movement of animals.\\n3. Plants were seasonal\\nDifferent plants produced fruits in different seasons.\\nPeople therefore moved from place to place depending on the availability of different plants.\\n4. Water was necessary\\nPeople, plants and animals all needed water.\\nWater could be found in:\\nLakes\\nStreams\\nRivers\\nSome water sources were seasonal, so people sometimes had to move in search of water during dry periods.\\n5. Meeting people\\nPeople may also have travelled to meet their friends and relatives.\\nImportantly, they travelled on foot.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Who Were Hunter-Gatherers?\\nQuestion:\\nWhy are early people described as hunter-gatherers?\\nA. They lived only by farming crops\\nB. They hunted animals and gathered food from nature\\nC. They lived permanently in large cities\\nD. They traded food with other communities\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nThey hunted wild animals, caught fish and birds, and gathered fruits, roots, nuts, seeds, leaves, stalks and eggs.\\nMCQ 2 — Why Did They Move?\\nQuestion:\\nA group has stayed in one place for a long time. Most of the available food resources are now used up. What would the group most likely do?\\nA. Stay there permanently\\nB. Stop eating food\\nC. Move to another place in search of food\\nD. Build a modern farm\\n✅ Correct Answer: C\\nExplanation:\\nHunter-gatherers moved when available plant and animal resources became limited. Animals also moved, plants produced fruit in different seasons, and water could become unavailable.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Should We Stay or Move?\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player reaches a hunter-gatherer camp.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Look around carefully. The group has been here for some time. Something has changed. Should they stay or move?”\\nThe player must inspect the environment and identify the clues.\\n🔎 Clues\\nThe player can interact with:\\n🍂 Empty food-gathering area\\n“Most nearby plant food has already been collected.”\\n🐾 Animal tracks\\n“The animals the group follows have moved away.”\\n🌱 Seasonal plants\\n“Some plants provide food during different seasons.”\\n💧 Dry water source\\n“This water source is no longer providing enough water.”\\n🏠 Comfortable shelter\\n“The shelter is still usable.”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nSelect all the clues that could explain why the group should move.\\nCorrect selections:\\n✅ 🍂 Food resources becoming limited\\n✅ 🐾 Animals moving away\\n✅ 🌱 Seasonal availability of plants\\n✅ 💧 Water becoming unavailable\\n❌ 🏠 Shelter is still usable\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Hunter-gatherers depended on nature for food and water. When resources changed or became limited, they moved to another place.”\\nThis works well as a puzzle because the player has to apply the information to an environment, rather than answer a direct question.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Empty Camp\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an old hunter-gatherer campsite.\\nThere is no one there.\\nAI Guide:\\n“Something happened here. The camp is empty, but there are clues all around you. Investigate them and find out why the group left.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nThe player must discover four clues.\\nClue 1 — 🍂 Food Area\\nThe player examines the nearby plants.\\nEvidence: Very little edible plant food remains.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The group may have used up much of the available plant food.”\\nClue 2 — 🐾 Animal Tracks\\nThe player follows animal tracks.\\nEvidence: The animals have moved away from the area.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The animals they hunted did not remain in one place.”\\nClue 3 — 💧 Water Source\\nThe player investigates the nearby water source.\\nEvidence: The water source is seasonal and currently has little/no water.\\nAI Guide:\\n“Water availability could also have forced the group to move.”\\nClue 4 — 🌿 Seasonal Plants\\nThe player examines plants from another area.\\nEvidence: Different plants provide food in different seasons.\\nAI Guide:\\n“The availability of plant foods changed with the seasons.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhy did the group leave?\\nA. They wanted to build a city\\nB. Their food, animals and water resources were changing or becoming limited\\nC. They had discovered machines\\nD. They wanted to travel by train\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The group depended on nature for survival. As food, animals, plants and water changed or became limited, they moved to another place.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
(4, 2, 1, 1, 'Puzzle — 🪨 Stone Tools & Technology', 'Puzzle game for Stage 2: 🪨 Stone Tools & Technology', 'Play the Puzzle activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 75, 15, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 2, \"title\": \"🪨 Stone Tools & Technology\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🪨 How Did Early People Make Tools?\\nEarly people made and used tools from different materials such as:\\n🪨 Stone\\n🪵 Wood\\n🦴 Bone\\nStone tools have survived especially well, so archaeologists can learn a lot about early people from them.\\n🔪 What Were Stone Tools Used For?\\nStone tools could be used for many different tasks.\\n🥩 Cutting meat and bone\\n🌳 Scraping bark from trees\\n🐾 Scraping animal hides\\n🍎 Chopping fruits and roots\\n🪵 Chopping wood\\n🔥 Collecting firewood\\n🌱 Digging the ground to collect edible roots\\n🏹 Making spears and arrows\\nSome stone tools could be attached to handles made of bone or wood to make spears and arrows for hunting.\\n🔨 How Were Stone Tools Made?\\nArchaeologists have identified two techniques that were probably used to make stone tools.\\n1. 🪨 Stone-on-Stone Technique\\nA stone called the core was held in one hand.\\nAnother stone was used as a hammer.\\nThe hammer stone was used to strike the core and remove pieces called flakes.\\nThe flakes could then be shaped into a tool.\\n🪨 CORE\\n+\\n🔨 HAMMER STONE\\n↓\\n💥 STRIKE\\n↓\\n🔹 FLAKES\\n↓\\n🪨 SHAPED TOOL\\n2. 🦴 Pressure Flaking\\nIn this technique, the core was placed on a firm surface.\\nA piece of bone or stone was placed against the core.\\nPressure was then used to remove flakes that could be shaped into tools.\\n🪨 CORE\\n↓\\n🦴 BONE / STONE\\n↓\\nPRESSURE\\n↓\\n🔹 FLAKES REMOVED\\n↓\\n🪨 TOOL\\n⏳ How Did Stone Tools Change Over Time?\\nArchaeologists divide this long period into different stages.\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\nThe Palaeolithic period extended from about 2 million years ago to about 12,000 years ago.\\nThe word refers to old stone, highlighting the importance of stone tools.\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago.\\nThe stone tools found from this period were generally very small and are called microliths.\\nMicroliths were probably attached to handles of bone or wood to make tools such as saws and sickles.\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago, the next stage is known as the Neolithic.\\nYou will learn more about the Neolithic in the next chapter.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Tool for the Task\\nQuestion:\\nA hunter-gatherer wants to cut meat and bone. Which type of tool would be most useful?\\nA. A stone tool\\nB. A clay pot\\nC. A wooden bowl\\nD. A basket\\n✅ Correct Answer: A\\nExplanation:\\nStone tools were used for tasks such as cutting meat and bone, scraping bark and hides, and chopping fruits and roots.\\nMCQ 2 — Making Stone Tools\\nQuestion:\\nIn the stone-on-stone technique, how were flakes removed from the core?\\nA. By heating the core in a fire\\nB. By striking the core with another stone\\nC. By soaking the core in water\\nD. By painting the core\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nIn the stone-on-stone technique, one stone was used as the core and another stone was used as a hammer to strike it and remove flakes.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Build the Right Tool\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric tool-making area.\\nSeveral objects are available:\\n🪨 Stone core\\n🔨 Hammer stone\\n🦴 Bone piece\\n🪵 Wooden handle\\n🍎 Fruit\\n🥩 Meat\\n🌱 Roots\\nAI Guide:\\n“These tools helped early people complete everyday tasks. Can you choose the right tools and materials for each job?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three tool-making/use challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — Cutting\\nTask: Choose the appropriate tool for cutting meat.\\nPlayer chooses:\\n🪨 Shaped stone tool\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — Hunting\\nTask: Create a hunting weapon using the available materials.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🪨 Stone tool\\n+\\n🪵 Wooden handle\\n→ 🏹 Spear\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — Tool Making\\nTask: Select the correct sequence for making a stone tool using the stone-on-stone technique.\\nA.\\nStone core → strike with another stone → remove flakes → shape tool\\nB.\\nStone core → paint it → place in water → tool\\nC.\\nStone core → bury it → wait → tool\\nD.\\nStone core → heat it → tool\\n✅ Correct: A\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Early people shaped stone into useful tools and used them for many different tasks. Some tools could also be attached to handles made of wood or bone.”\\nThe puzzle is about applying the knowledge rather than simply remembering a definition.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Broken Tool\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player discovers an old prehistoric activity area.\\nOn the ground are:\\n🪨 A stone core\\n🔨 A hammer stone\\n🔹 Several stone flakes\\n🏹 A damaged hunting tool\\n🪵 A wooden handle\\nAI Guide:\\n“Someone was making tools here, but something went wrong. Investigate the evidence and discover how the tool was made.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🪨 The Core\\nThe player examines the large stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“This stone shows signs of being struck.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This may have been the core from which flakes were removed.”\\nClue 2 — 🔨 The Hammer Stone\\nThe player examines another stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“This stone could have been used to strike the core.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“It looks like a hammer stone.”\\nClue 3 — 🔹 The Flakes\\nThe player examines the small pieces of stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Several flakes have been removed from the core.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“These flakes could have been shaped into tools.”\\nClue 4 — 🏹 The Damaged Tool\\nThe player examines the damaged hunting tool.\\nEvidence:\\n“A shaped stone is attached to a wooden handle.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Some stone tools were attached to handles of wood or bone to make spears and arrows.”\\nThis is consistent with the NCERT description of stone tools and their uses.\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nHow was this tool probably made?\\nA. The stone was painted and placed in water.\\nB. A stone core was struck to remove flakes, which were shaped into a tool.\\nC. The stone was buried until it naturally became a tool.\\nD. The stone was made from clay and baked.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The evidence suggests that the tool was made by removing flakes from a stone core and shaping them. Early people developed techniques for making useful stone tools, and some were attached to wood or bone handles.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
