Wooden handle\\n→ 🏹 Spear\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — Tool Making\\nTask: Select the correct sequence for making a stone tool using the stone-on-stone technique.\\nA.\\nStone core → strike with another stone → remove flakes → shape tool\\nB.\\nStone core → paint it → place in water → tool\\nC.\\nStone core → bury it → wait → tool\\nD.\\nStone core → heat it → tool\\n✅ Correct: A\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Early people shaped stone into useful tools and used them for many different tasks. Some tools could also be attached to handles made of wood or bone.”\\nThe puzzle is about applying the knowledge rather than simply remembering a definition.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Broken Tool\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player discovers an old prehistoric activity area.\\nOn the ground are:\\n🪨 A stone core\\n🔨 A hammer stone\\n🔹 Several stone flakes\\n🏹 A damaged hunting tool\\n🪵 A wooden handle\\nAI Guide:\\n“Someone was making tools here, but something went wrong. Investigate the evidence and discover how the tool was made.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🪨 The Core\\nThe player examines the large stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“This stone shows signs of being struck.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This may have been the core from which flakes were removed.”\\nClue 2 — 🔨 The Hammer Stone\\nThe player examines another stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“This stone could have been used to strike the core.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“It looks like a hammer stone.”\\nClue 3 — 🔹 The Flakes\\nThe player examines the small pieces of stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Several flakes have been removed from the core.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“These flakes could have been shaped into tools.”\\nClue 4 — 🏹 The Damaged Tool\\nThe player examines the damaged hunting tool.\\nEvidence:\\n“A shaped stone is attached to a wooden handle.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Some stone tools were attached to handles of wood or bone to make spears and arrows.”\\nThis is consistent with the NCERT description of stone tools and their uses.\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nHow was this tool probably made?\\nA. The stone was painted and placed in water.\\nB. A stone core was struck to remove flakes, which were shaped into a tool.\\nC. The stone was buried until it naturally became a tool.\\nD. The stone was made from clay and baked.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The evidence suggests that the tool was made by removing flakes from a stone core and shaping them. Early people developed techniques for making useful stone tools, and some were attached to wood or bone handles.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
(6, 2, 3, 3, 'Theoretical — 🪨 Stone Tools & Technology', 'Theoretical game for Stage 2: 🪨 Stone Tools & Technology', 'Play the Theoretical activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 50, 10, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 2, \"title\": \"🪨 Stone Tools & Technology\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🪨 How Did Early People Make Tools?\\nEarly people made and used tools from different materials such as:\\n🪨 Stone\\n🪵 Wood\\n🦴 Bone\\nStone tools have survived especially well, so archaeologists can learn a lot about early people from them.\\n🔪 What Were Stone Tools Used For?\\nStone tools could be used for many different tasks.\\n🥩 Cutting meat and bone\\n🌳 Scraping bark from trees\\n🐾 Scraping animal hides\\n🍎 Chopping fruits and roots\\n🪵 Chopping wood\\n🔥 Collecting firewood\\n🌱 Digging the ground to collect edible roots\\n🏹 Making spears and arrows\\nSome stone tools could be attached to handles made of bone or wood to make spears and arrows for hunting.\\n🔨 How Were Stone Tools Made?\\nArchaeologists have identified two techniques that were probably used to make stone tools.\\n1. 🪨 Stone-on-Stone Technique\\nA stone called the core was held in one hand.\\nAnother stone was used as a hammer.\\nThe hammer stone was used to strike the core and remove pieces called flakes.\\nThe flakes could then be shaped into a tool.\\n🪨 CORE\\n+\\n🔨 HAMMER STONE\\n↓\\n💥 STRIKE\\n↓\\n🔹 FLAKES\\n↓\\n🪨 SHAPED TOOL\\n2. 🦴 Pressure Flaking\\nIn this technique, the core was placed on a firm surface.\\nA piece of bone or stone was placed against the core.\\nPressure was then used to remove flakes that could be shaped into tools.\\n🪨 CORE\\n↓\\n🦴 BONE / STONE\\n↓\\nPRESSURE\\n↓\\n🔹 FLAKES REMOVED\\n↓\\n🪨 TOOL\\n⏳ How Did Stone Tools Change Over Time?\\nArchaeologists divide this long period into different stages.\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\nThe Palaeolithic period extended from about 2 million years ago to about 12,000 years ago.\\nThe word refers to old stone, highlighting the importance of stone tools.\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago.\\nThe stone tools found from this period were generally very small and are called microliths.\\nMicroliths were probably attached to handles of bone or wood to make tools such as saws and sickles.\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago, the next stage is known as the Neolithic.\\nYou will learn more about the Neolithic in the next chapter.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Tool for the Task\\nQuestion:\\nA hunter-gatherer wants to cut meat and bone. Which type of tool would be most useful?\\nA. A stone tool\\nB. A clay pot\\nC. A wooden bowl\\nD. A basket\\n✅ Correct Answer: A\\nExplanation:\\nStone tools were used for tasks such as cutting meat and bone, scraping bark and hides, and chopping fruits and roots.\\nMCQ 2 — Making Stone Tools\\nQuestion:\\nIn the stone-on-stone technique, how were flakes removed from the core?\\nA. By heating the core in a fire\\nB. By striking the core with another stone\\nC. By soaking the core in water\\nD. By painting the core\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nIn the stone-on-stone technique, one stone was used as the core and another stone was used as a hammer to strike it and remove flakes.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Build the Right Tool\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric tool-making area.\\nSeveral objects are available:\\n🪨 Stone core\\n🔨 Hammer stone\\n🦴 Bone piece\\n🪵 Wooden handle\\n🍎 Fruit\\n🥩 Meat\\n🌱 Roots\\nAI Guide:\\n“These tools helped early people complete everyday tasks. Can you choose the right tools and materials for each job?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three tool-making/use challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — Cutting\\nTask: Choose the appropriate tool for cutting meat.\\nPlayer chooses:\\n🪨 Shaped stone tool\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — Hunting\\nTask: Create a hunting weapon using the available materials.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🪨 Stone tool\\n+\\n🪵 Wooden handle\\n→ 🏹 Spear\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — Tool Making\\nTask: Select the correct sequence for making a stone tool using the stone-on-stone technique.\\nA.\\nStone core → strike with another stone → remove flakes → shape tool\\nB.\\nStone core → paint it → place in water → tool\\nC.\\nStone core → bury it → wait → tool\\nD.\\nStone core → heat it → tool\\n✅ Correct: A\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Early people shaped stone into useful tools and used them for many different tasks. Some tools could also be attached to handles made of wood or bone.”\\nThe puzzle is about applying the knowledge rather than simply remembering a definition.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Broken Tool\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player discovers an old prehistoric activity area.\\nOn the ground are:\\n🪨 A stone core\\n🔨 A hammer stone\\n🔹 Several stone flakes\\n🏹 A damaged hunting tool\\n🪵 A wooden handle\\nAI Guide:\\n“Someone was making tools here, but something went wrong. Investigate the evidence and discover how the tool was made.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🪨 The Core\\nThe player examines the large stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“This stone shows signs of being struck.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This may have been the core from which flakes were removed.”\\nClue 2 — 🔨 The Hammer Stone\\nThe player examines another stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“This stone could have been used to strike the core.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“It looks like a hammer stone.”\\nClue 3 — 🔹 The Flakes\\nThe player examines the small pieces of stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Several flakes have been removed from the core.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“These flakes could have been shaped into tools.”\\nClue 4 — 🏹 The Damaged Tool\\nThe player examines the damaged hunting tool.\\nEvidence:\\n“A shaped stone is attached to a wooden handle.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Some stone tools were attached to handles of wood or bone to make spears and arrows.”\\nThis is consistent with the NCERT description of stone tools and their uses.\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nHow was this tool probably made?\\nA. The stone was painted and placed in water.\\nB. A stone core was struck to remove flakes, which were shaped into a tool.\\nC. The stone was buried until it naturally became a tool.\\nD. The stone was made from clay and baked.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The evidence suggests that the tool was made by removing flakes from a stone core and shaping them. Early people developed techniques for making useful stone tools, and some were attached to wood or bone handles.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
(7, 3, 1, 1, 'Puzzle — 🔥 Fire & Survival', 'Puzzle game for Stage 3: 🔥 Fire & Survival', 'Play the Puzzle activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 75, 15, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 3, \"title\": \"🔥 Fire & Survival\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🔥 How Did Early People Use Fire?\\nEarly people were familiar with the use of fire.\\nTraces of ash have been found at the Kurnool caves. This suggests that people living there were familiar with the use of fire.\\nFire could have been an important part of life for early hunter-gatherers.\\n💡 What Was Fire Used For?\\nFire could have been used for several important purposes.\\n💡 Source of light\\nFire provided light in dark surroundings, including caves and rock shelters.\\n🍖 Roasting meat\\nFire could be used to roast meat.\\n🐾 Scaring away animals\\nFire could be used to scare away animals.\\nSo, fire could help early people with:\\n💡 Light\\n🍖 Food\\n🐾 Protection\\nThese uses are specifically mentioned in the NCERT chapter.\\n🪨 How Do We Know Early People Used Fire?\\nArchaeologists learn about the past by studying evidence left behind by people.\\nOne important piece of evidence is the discovery of traces of ash at the Kurnool caves.\\n🔎 Kurnool Caves\\n⬇️\\n⚪ Traces of ash found\\n⬇️\\n🔥 Evidence of fire\\n⬇️\\n🧑‍🔬 Archaeologists study the evidence\\n⬇️\\n📖 We learn that people were familiar with fire\\nThe ash at Kurnool suggests that people there were familiar with the use of fire.\\n🌍 How Did the Environment Change?\\nAround 12,000 years ago, major changes took place in the world\'s climate.\\nThe climate shifted to relatively warmer conditions.\\nIn many areas, this led to the development of grasslands.\\n🌡️ Warmer climate\\n⬇️\\n🌱 Grasslands developed\\n⬇️\\n🦌 The environment changed\\n⬇️\\n🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Early people had to adapt to a changing environment\\nThis environmental change is important because the lives of early people were closely connected with their surroundings.\\n🔥 Why Was Fire Important for Survival?\\nFire could help early people in several ways.\\n💡 Light\\nHelped them see in dark surroundings.\\n🍖 Food\\nHelped them roast meat.\\n🐾 Protection\\nCould scare away animals.\\n🔥 FIRE\\nLIGHT → FOOD → PROTECTION\\nThe discovery of ash at Kurnool gives archaeologists evidence about the use of fire by early people.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🔥 Early people were familiar with fire.\\n🪨 Traces of ash were found at the Kurnool caves.\\n💡 Fire could be used as a source of light.\\n🍖 Fire could be used to roast meat.\\n🐾 Fire could be used to scare away animals.\\n🌍 Around 12,000 years ago, the climate became relatively warmer and grasslands developed.\\n🔎 Archaeologists use evidence such as ash to learn about the past.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Uses of Fire\\nQuestion:\\nWhich of the following was a use of fire by early people?\\nA. Making electricity\\nB. Roasting meat\\nC. Running machines\\nD. Making plastic\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nFire could have been used as a source of light, to roast meat, and to scare away animals.\\nMCQ 2 — Evidence of Fire\\nQuestion:\\nWhat evidence found at the Kurnool caves suggests that early people were familiar with fire?\\nA. Traces of ash\\nB. Written records\\nC. Metal tools\\nD. Brick houses\\n✅ Correct Answer: A\\nExplanation:\\nTraces of ash were found at the Kurnool caves. This suggests that people there were familiar with the use of fire.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Prepare the Fire Camp\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric campsite just before night.\\nThere is a fire area and several objects around the camp.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“The sun is going down. Fire could help this group in more than one way. Can you prepare the camp correctly?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must use the fire for three appropriate purposes.\\nChallenge 1 — 💡 Light\\nTask:\\nThe cave is becoming dark. What should the player use?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — 🍖 Food\\nTask:\\nThe group needs to prepare meat. What can they use?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire + Meat\\n→ 🍖 Roasted Meat\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — 🐾 Protection\\nTask:\\nAnimals are approaching the camp. What could help scare them away?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire\\n→ 🐾 Animals move away\\n✅ Correct.\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Fire could provide light, help people roast meat, and scare away animals. You used the fire just as early people may have used it.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to apply the different uses of fire to situations, rather than simply remembering them.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Ashes of Kurnool\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient cave.\\nThe area appears to have been used by early people.\\nOn the ground, the player discovers:\\n⚪ A patch of ash\\n🪨 Stone tools\\n🕳️ A fire area\\n🏕️ Signs of an old activity area\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“This cave contains clues from the past. Investigate the evidence and discover what happened here.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nThe player must discover four clues.\\nClue 1 — ⚪ The Ash\\nThe player examines the ash.\\nEvidence:\\n“Traces of ash remain on the ground.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Ash can be evidence that a fire was present here.”\\nClue 2 — 🔥 The Fire Area\\nThe player examines the area where the ash was found.\\nEvidence:\\n“The ash is concentrated in one area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This could have been a place where fire was used.”\\nClue 3 — 🌙 The Cave\\nThe player looks around the dark cave.\\nEvidence:\\n“The cave would have been dark without a source of light.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Fire could have been useful as a source of light.”\\nClue 4 — 🐾 Animal Threat\\nThe player notices animal tracks near the cave entrance.\\nEvidence:\\n“Animals may have approached the area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Fire could also have been used to scare away animals.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat does the evidence at the cave suggest?\\nA. People built a modern kitchen.\\nB. People were familiar with the use of fire.\\nC. People used electricity inside the cave.\\nD. People lived in a permanent city.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The traces of ash at Kurnool suggest that early people were familiar with fire. Fire could provide light, help roast meat, and scare away animals.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56');
