✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — 🍖 Food\\nTask:\\nThe group needs to prepare meat. What can they use?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire + Meat\\n→ 🍖 Roasted Meat\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — 🐾 Protection\\nTask:\\nAnimals are approaching the camp. What could help scare them away?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire\\n→ 🐾 Animals move away\\n✅ Correct.\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Fire could provide light, help people roast meat, and scare away animals. You used the fire just as early people may have used it.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to apply the different uses of fire to situations, rather than simply remembering them.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Ashes of Kurnool\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient cave.\\nThe area appears to have been used by early people.\\nOn the ground, the player discovers:\\n⚪ A patch of ash\\n🪨 Stone tools\\n🕳️ A fire area\\n🏕️ Signs of an old activity area\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“This cave contains clues from the past. Investigate the evidence and discover what happened here.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nThe player must discover four clues.\\nClue 1 — ⚪ The Ash\\nThe player examines the ash.\\nEvidence:\\n“Traces of ash remain on the ground.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Ash can be evidence that a fire was present here.”\\nClue 2 — 🔥 The Fire Area\\nThe player examines the area where the ash was found.\\nEvidence:\\n“The ash is concentrated in one area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This could have been a place where fire was used.”\\nClue 3 — 🌙 The Cave\\nThe player looks around the dark cave.\\nEvidence:\\n“The cave would have been dark without a source of light.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Fire could have been useful as a source of light.”\\nClue 4 — 🐾 Animal Threat\\nThe player notices animal tracks near the cave entrance.\\nEvidence:\\n“Animals may have approached the area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Fire could also have been used to scare away animals.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat does the evidence at the cave suggest?\\nA. People built a modern kitchen.\\nB. People were familiar with the use of fire.\\nC. People used electricity inside the cave.\\nD. People lived in a permanent city.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The traces of ash at Kurnool suggest that early people were familiar with fire. Fire could provide light, help roast meat, and scare away animals.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
(9, 3, 3, 3, 'Theoretical — 🔥 Fire & Survival', 'Theoretical game for Stage 3: 🔥 Fire & Survival', 'Play the Theoretical activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 50, 10, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 3, \"title\": \"🔥 Fire & Survival\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🔥 How Did Early People Use Fire?\\nEarly people were familiar with the use of fire.\\nTraces of ash have been found at the Kurnool caves. This suggests that people living there were familiar with the use of fire.\\nFire could have been an important part of life for early hunter-gatherers.\\n💡 What Was Fire Used For?\\nFire could have been used for several important purposes.\\n💡 Source of light\\nFire provided light in dark surroundings, including caves and rock shelters.\\n🍖 Roasting meat\\nFire could be used to roast meat.\\n🐾 Scaring away animals\\nFire could be used to scare away animals.\\nSo, fire could help early people with:\\n💡 Light\\n🍖 Food\\n🐾 Protection\\nThese uses are specifically mentioned in the NCERT chapter.\\n🪨 How Do We Know Early People Used Fire?\\nArchaeologists learn about the past by studying evidence left behind by people.\\nOne important piece of evidence is the discovery of traces of ash at the Kurnool caves.\\n🔎 Kurnool Caves\\n⬇️\\n⚪ Traces of ash found\\n⬇️\\n🔥 Evidence of fire\\n⬇️\\n🧑‍🔬 Archaeologists study the evidence\\n⬇️\\n📖 We learn that people were familiar with fire\\nThe ash at Kurnool suggests that people there were familiar with the use of fire.\\n🌍 How Did the Environment Change?\\nAround 12,000 years ago, major changes took place in the world\'s climate.\\nThe climate shifted to relatively warmer conditions.\\nIn many areas, this led to the development of grasslands.\\n🌡️ Warmer climate\\n⬇️\\n🌱 Grasslands developed\\n⬇️\\n🦌 The environment changed\\n⬇️\\n🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Early people had to adapt to a changing environment\\nThis environmental change is important because the lives of early people were closely connected with their surroundings.\\n🔥 Why Was Fire Important for Survival?\\nFire could help early people in several ways.\\n💡 Light\\nHelped them see in dark surroundings.\\n🍖 Food\\nHelped them roast meat.\\n🐾 Protection\\nCould scare away animals.\\n🔥 FIRE\\nLIGHT → FOOD → PROTECTION\\nThe discovery of ash at Kurnool gives archaeologists evidence about the use of fire by early people.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🔥 Early people were familiar with fire.\\n🪨 Traces of ash were found at the Kurnool caves.\\n💡 Fire could be used as a source of light.\\n🍖 Fire could be used to roast meat.\\n🐾 Fire could be used to scare away animals.\\n🌍 Around 12,000 years ago, the climate became relatively warmer and grasslands developed.\\n🔎 Archaeologists use evidence such as ash to learn about the past.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Uses of Fire\\nQuestion:\\nWhich of the following was a use of fire by early people?\\nA. Making electricity\\nB. Roasting meat\\nC. Running machines\\nD. Making plastic\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nFire could have been used as a source of light, to roast meat, and to scare away animals.\\nMCQ 2 — Evidence of Fire\\nQuestion:\\nWhat evidence found at the Kurnool caves suggests that early people were familiar with fire?\\nA. Traces of ash\\nB. Written records\\nC. Metal tools\\nD. Brick houses\\n✅ Correct Answer: A\\nExplanation:\\nTraces of ash were found at the Kurnool caves. This suggests that people there were familiar with the use of fire.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Prepare the Fire Camp\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric campsite just before night.\\nThere is a fire area and several objects around the camp.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“The sun is going down. Fire could help this group in more than one way. Can you prepare the camp correctly?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must use the fire for three appropriate purposes.\\nChallenge 1 — 💡 Light\\nTask:\\nThe cave is becoming dark. What should the player use?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — 🍖 Food\\nTask:\\nThe group needs to prepare meat. What can they use?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire + Meat\\n→ 🍖 Roasted Meat\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — 🐾 Protection\\nTask:\\nAnimals are approaching the camp. What could help scare them away?\\nPlayer selects:\\n🔥 Fire\\n→ 🐾 Animals move away\\n✅ Correct.\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! Fire could provide light, help people roast meat, and scare away animals. You used the fire just as early people may have used it.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to apply the different uses of fire to situations, rather than simply remembering them.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Ashes of Kurnool\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient cave.\\nThe area appears to have been used by early people.\\nOn the ground, the player discovers:\\n⚪ A patch of ash\\n🪨 Stone tools\\n🕳️ A fire area\\n🏕️ Signs of an old activity area\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“This cave contains clues from the past. Investigate the evidence and discover what happened here.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nThe player must discover four clues.\\nClue 1 — ⚪ The Ash\\nThe player examines the ash.\\nEvidence:\\n“Traces of ash remain on the ground.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Ash can be evidence that a fire was present here.”\\nClue 2 — 🔥 The Fire Area\\nThe player examines the area where the ash was found.\\nEvidence:\\n“The ash is concentrated in one area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This could have been a place where fire was used.”\\nClue 3 — 🌙 The Cave\\nThe player looks around the dark cave.\\nEvidence:\\n“The cave would have been dark without a source of light.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Fire could have been useful as a source of light.”\\nClue 4 — 🐾 Animal Threat\\nThe player notices animal tracks near the cave entrance.\\nEvidence:\\n“Animals may have approached the area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Fire could also have been used to scare away animals.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat does the evidence at the cave suggest?\\nA. People built a modern kitchen.\\nB. People were familiar with the use of fire.\\nC. People used electricity inside the cave.\\nD. People lived in a permanent city.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The traces of ash at Kurnool suggest that early people were familiar with fire. Fire could provide light, help roast meat, and scare away animals.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:56', '2026-09-10 04:34:56'),
(10, 4, 1, 1, 'Puzzle — 🗺️ Archaeological Sites & Prehistoric Timeline', 'Puzzle game for Stage 4: 🗺️ Archaeological Sites & Prehistoric Timeline', 'Play the Puzzle activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 75, 15, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 4, \"title\": \"🗺️ Archaeological Sites & Prehistoric Timeline\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🪨 What Are Archaeological Sites?\\nArchaeological sites are places where remains of things made, used, and left behind by people are found.\\nThese remains can include:\\n🪨 Tools\\n🏺 Pots\\n🏠 Buildings\\nThese remains may be found:\\n🌍 On the surface of the earth\\n⛏️ Buried under the earth\\n🌊 Sometimes even under water\\nArchaeologists study these remains to learn about people who lived in the past.\\n🏠 What Are Habitation Sites?\\nSome archaeological sites are places where people lived. These are called habitation sites.\\nThey include:\\n🪨 Caves\\n⛰️ Rock shelters\\nPeople chose natural caves and rock shelters because they provided protection from:\\n🌧️ Rain\\n☀️ Heat\\n💨 Wind\\nNatural caves and rock shelters are found in areas such as the Vindhyas and the Deccan Plateau, including areas close to the Narmada valley.\\n🏕️ Habitation Site\\nPeople lived here\\n⬇️\\n🪨 Caves / Rock Shelters\\n⬇️\\n🌧️ ☀️ 💨\\nProtection from rain, heat and wind\\n🏭 What Are Factory Sites?\\nEarly people needed good-quality stone to make tools.\\nPlaces where suitable stone was available and where people made tools are called factory sites.\\nArchaeologists may find:\\n🪨 Blocks of stone\\n🔨 Tools\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🗑️ Waste pieces of stone\\nThese remains can help archaeologists identify places where tools were made.\\n🏭 What Are Habitation-cum-Factory Sites?\\nSometimes people not only made tools at a site but also lived there for longer periods.\\nSuch places are called:\\n🏠 Habitation-cum-factory sites\\nThey combined two activities:\\n🏠 Habitation → People lived there\\n🏭 Factory → People made tools there\\n→ 🏠🏭 Habitation-cum-factory\\n⏳ How Do Archaeologists Divide Prehistory?\\nArchaeologists have given names to different periods of early human history.\\nThe earliest period discussed here is called:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\nMeaning: Old Stone\\nThe Palaeolithic period extended from about:\\n2 million years ago → 12,000 years ago\\nIt is divided into:\\n🔹 Lower Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Middle Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Upper Palaeolithic\\nThe name highlights the importance of stone tools found from this period.\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted approximately:\\n12,000 years ago → 10,000 years ago\\nThe stone tools from this period were generally very small.\\nThese small tools are called:\\n🔹 Microliths\\nMicroliths were probably attached to handles made of:\\n🦴 Bone\\n🪵 Wood\\nThey could be used to make tools such as:\\n🪚 Saws\\n🌾 Sickles\\nOlder varieties of tools continued to be used during this period as well.\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago, the next stage is known as the Neolithic.\\nThe Neolithic period will be studied in the next chapter.\\n⏳ PREHISTORIC TIMELINE\\n2 million years ago\\n⬇️\\n🪨 PALAEOLITHIC\\n2 million → 12,000 years ago\\n⬇️\\n🔹 MESOLITHIC\\n12,000 → 10,000 years ago\\n⬇️\\n🌱 NEOLITHIC\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago\\n🗺️ Important Archaeological Sites\\nSome important places mentioned in the chapter include:\\n📍 Bhimbetka — a habitation site with caves and rock shelters in present-day Madhya Pradesh.\\n📍 Hunsgi — an important prehistoric site associated with stone tools.\\n📍 Kurnool Caves — where traces of ash provide evidence of familiarity with fire.\\nThese places help archaeologists understand different aspects of early human life.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🪨 Archaeological sites contain remains left behind by people.\\n🏠 Habitation sites were places where people lived.\\n🏭 Factory sites were places where people made stone tools.\\n🏠🏭 Habitation-cum-factory sites were used for both living and making tools.\\n🪨 Palaeolithic: about 2 million → 12,000 years ago.\\n🔹 Mesolithic: about 12,000 → 10,000 years ago.\\n🔹 Microliths: tiny stone tools of the Mesolithic period.\\n🌱 Neolithic: from about 10,000 years ago.\\n📍 Archaeological sites such as Bhimbetka, Hunsgi and Kurnool provide evidence about early people.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Understanding Sites\\nQuestion:\\nWhat is an archaeological site?\\nA. A modern museum\\nB. A place where remains left behind by people are found\\nC. A place where only animals live\\nD. A modern factory\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nArchaeological sites are places where remains such as tools, pots and buildings made, used and left behind by people are found.\\nMCQ 2 — Prehistoric Timeline\\nQuestion:\\nWhich period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago?\\nA. Palaeolithic\\nB. Mesolithic\\nC. Neolithic\\nD. Modern period\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago. The tiny stone tools found from this period are called microliths.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Discover the Prehistoric Site\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters an unexplored prehistoric landscape.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“We have discovered a place left behind by early people. Look carefully at the evidence. Can you figure out what happened here and when?”\\nThe player sees several areas:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🏠 Rock shelter\\n🪵 Tool-making area\\n⏳ Timeline markers\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three investigation challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — 🏠 Identify the Site\\nTask:\\nThe player finds a natural rock shelter.\\nWhat was this type of place most likely used for?\\nA. Living\\nB. Making modern machines\\nC. Storing cars\\nD. Building roads\\nPlayer selects:\\n🏠 Living\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — 🏭 Identify the Factory\\nThe player discovers:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔨 Tool-making area\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\nTask:\\nWhat type of site does this evidence suggest?\\nA. Habitation site\\nB. Factory site\\nC. Modern village\\nD. Burial site\\nPlayer selects:\\n🏭 Factory site\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — ⏳ Build the Timeline\\nThe player receives three timeline cards:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nThe player must arrange them in the correct order.\\nCorrect Sequence:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\n↓\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\n↓\\n🌱 Neolithic\\n✅ Correct.\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! You used the evidence to identify the site and then placed the prehistoric periods in the correct order. Archaeologists use remains and dates to build our understanding of the past.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to identify evidence, classify a site and apply the prehistoric timeline, rather than simply memorising definitions.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Site That Tells a Story\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient archaeological site.\\nThere are no people there, but many objects remain.\\nThe player discovers:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🏠 Rock shelter\\n🔨 Tool-making area\\n⏳ Ancient timeline marker\\nAI Guide:\\n“This place has no written story, but the remains left behind may tell us what happened here. Investigate the evidence.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🏠 The Rock Shelter\\nThe player examines the shelter.\\nEvidence:\\n“The natural shelter could have protected people from rain, heat and wind.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This could have been a habitation site where people lived.”\\nClue 2 — 🪨 The Stone Blocks\\nThe player examines large pieces of stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Good-quality stone is available at this location.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Early people looked for places where suitable stone was easily available for making tools.”\\nClue 3 — 🔹 The Stone Flakes\\nThe player discovers many small pieces of broken stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Chips and waste pieces of stone are scattered around the area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“These remains can be evidence that stone tools were being made here.”\\nClue 4 — ⏳ The Timeline\\nThe player finds evidence associated with very small stone tools.\\nEvidence:\\n“The tools are tiny and may be microliths.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Microliths were characteristic of the Mesolithic period, which lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat does the evidence suggest about this place?\\nA. It was a modern industrial factory.\\nB. It was a prehistoric place where people lived and made stone tools.\\nC. It was a modern city.\\nD. It was a railway station.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The shelter suggests that people lived here, while the stone blocks and flakes suggest that tools were made here. The evidence helps archaeologists reconstruct how people lived in the distant past.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:57', '2026-09-10 04:34:57'),
