\\n→ 🏠🏭 Habitation-cum-factory\\n⏳ How Do Archaeologists Divide Prehistory?\\nArchaeologists have given names to different periods of early human history.\\nThe earliest period discussed here is called:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\nMeaning: Old Stone\\nThe Palaeolithic period extended from about:\\n2 million years ago → 12,000 years ago\\nIt is divided into:\\n🔹 Lower Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Middle Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Upper Palaeolithic\\nThe name highlights the importance of stone tools found from this period.\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted approximately:\\n12,000 years ago → 10,000 years ago\\nThe stone tools from this period were generally very small.\\nThese small tools are called:\\n🔹 Microliths\\nMicroliths were probably attached to handles made of:\\n🦴 Bone\\n🪵 Wood\\nThey could be used to make tools such as:\\n🪚 Saws\\n🌾 Sickles\\nOlder varieties of tools continued to be used during this period as well.\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago, the next stage is known as the Neolithic.\\nThe Neolithic period will be studied in the next chapter.\\n⏳ PREHISTORIC TIMELINE\\n2 million years ago\\n⬇️\\n🪨 PALAEOLITHIC\\n2 million → 12,000 years ago\\n⬇️\\n🔹 MESOLITHIC\\n12,000 → 10,000 years ago\\n⬇️\\n🌱 NEOLITHIC\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago\\n🗺️ Important Archaeological Sites\\nSome important places mentioned in the chapter include:\\n📍 Bhimbetka — a habitation site with caves and rock shelters in present-day Madhya Pradesh.\\n📍 Hunsgi — an important prehistoric site associated with stone tools.\\n📍 Kurnool Caves — where traces of ash provide evidence of familiarity with fire.\\nThese places help archaeologists understand different aspects of early human life.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🪨 Archaeological sites contain remains left behind by people.\\n🏠 Habitation sites were places where people lived.\\n🏭 Factory sites were places where people made stone tools.\\n🏠🏭 Habitation-cum-factory sites were used for both living and making tools.\\n🪨 Palaeolithic: about 2 million → 12,000 years ago.\\n🔹 Mesolithic: about 12,000 → 10,000 years ago.\\n🔹 Microliths: tiny stone tools of the Mesolithic period.\\n🌱 Neolithic: from about 10,000 years ago.\\n📍 Archaeological sites such as Bhimbetka, Hunsgi and Kurnool provide evidence about early people.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Understanding Sites\\nQuestion:\\nWhat is an archaeological site?\\nA. A modern museum\\nB. A place where remains left behind by people are found\\nC. A place where only animals live\\nD. A modern factory\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nArchaeological sites are places where remains such as tools, pots and buildings made, used and left behind by people are found.\\nMCQ 2 — Prehistoric Timeline\\nQuestion:\\nWhich period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago?\\nA. Palaeolithic\\nB. Mesolithic\\nC. Neolithic\\nD. Modern period\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago. The tiny stone tools found from this period are called microliths.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Discover the Prehistoric Site\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters an unexplored prehistoric landscape.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“We have discovered a place left behind by early people. Look carefully at the evidence. Can you figure out what happened here and when?”\\nThe player sees several areas:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🏠 Rock shelter\\n🪵 Tool-making area\\n⏳ Timeline markers\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three investigation challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — 🏠 Identify the Site\\nTask:\\nThe player finds a natural rock shelter.\\nWhat was this type of place most likely used for?\\nA. Living\\nB. Making modern machines\\nC. Storing cars\\nD. Building roads\\nPlayer selects:\\n🏠 Living\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — 🏭 Identify the Factory\\nThe player discovers:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔨 Tool-making area\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\nTask:\\nWhat type of site does this evidence suggest?\\nA. Habitation site\\nB. Factory site\\nC. Modern village\\nD. Burial site\\nPlayer selects:\\n🏭 Factory site\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — ⏳ Build the Timeline\\nThe player receives three timeline cards:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nThe player must arrange them in the correct order.\\nCorrect Sequence:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\n↓\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\n↓\\n🌱 Neolithic\\n✅ Correct.\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! You used the evidence to identify the site and then placed the prehistoric periods in the correct order. Archaeologists use remains and dates to build our understanding of the past.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to identify evidence, classify a site and apply the prehistoric timeline, rather than simply memorising definitions.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Site That Tells a Story\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient archaeological site.\\nThere are no people there, but many objects remain.\\nThe player discovers:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🏠 Rock shelter\\n🔨 Tool-making area\\n⏳ Ancient timeline marker\\nAI Guide:\\n“This place has no written story, but the remains left behind may tell us what happened here. Investigate the evidence.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🏠 The Rock Shelter\\nThe player examines the shelter.\\nEvidence:\\n“The natural shelter could have protected people from rain, heat and wind.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This could have been a habitation site where people lived.”\\nClue 2 — 🪨 The Stone Blocks\\nThe player examines large pieces of stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Good-quality stone is available at this location.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Early people looked for places where suitable stone was easily available for making tools.”\\nClue 3 — 🔹 The Stone Flakes\\nThe player discovers many small pieces of broken stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Chips and waste pieces of stone are scattered around the area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“These remains can be evidence that stone tools were being made here.”\\nClue 4 — ⏳ The Timeline\\nThe player finds evidence associated with very small stone tools.\\nEvidence:\\n“The tools are tiny and may be microliths.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Microliths were characteristic of the Mesolithic period, which lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat does the evidence suggest about this place?\\nA. It was a modern industrial factory.\\nB. It was a prehistoric place where people lived and made stone tools.\\nC. It was a modern city.\\nD. It was a railway station.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The shelter suggests that people lived here, while the stone blocks and flakes suggest that tools were made here. The evidence helps archaeologists reconstruct how people lived in the distant past.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:57', '2026-09-10 04:34:57'),
(12, 4, 3, 3, 'Theoretical — 🗺️ Archaeological Sites & Prehistoric Timeline', 'Theoretical game for Stage 4: 🗺️ Archaeological Sites & Prehistoric Timeline', 'Play the Theoretical activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 50, 10, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 4, \"title\": \"🗺️ Archaeological Sites & Prehistoric Timeline\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🪨 What Are Archaeological Sites?\\nArchaeological sites are places where remains of things made, used, and left behind by people are found.\\nThese remains can include:\\n🪨 Tools\\n🏺 Pots\\n🏠 Buildings\\nThese remains may be found:\\n🌍 On the surface of the earth\\n⛏️ Buried under the earth\\n🌊 Sometimes even under water\\nArchaeologists study these remains to learn about people who lived in the past.\\n🏠 What Are Habitation Sites?\\nSome archaeological sites are places where people lived. These are called habitation sites.\\nThey include:\\n🪨 Caves\\n⛰️ Rock shelters\\nPeople chose natural caves and rock shelters because they provided protection from:\\n🌧️ Rain\\n☀️ Heat\\n💨 Wind\\nNatural caves and rock shelters are found in areas such as the Vindhyas and the Deccan Plateau, including areas close to the Narmada valley.\\n🏕️ Habitation Site\\nPeople lived here\\n⬇️\\n🪨 Caves / Rock Shelters\\n⬇️\\n🌧️ ☀️ 💨\\nProtection from rain, heat and wind\\n🏭 What Are Factory Sites?\\nEarly people needed good-quality stone to make tools.\\nPlaces where suitable stone was available and where people made tools are called factory sites.\\nArchaeologists may find:\\n🪨 Blocks of stone\\n🔨 Tools\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🗑️ Waste pieces of stone\\nThese remains can help archaeologists identify places where tools were made.\\n🏭 What Are Habitation-cum-Factory Sites?\\nSometimes people not only made tools at a site but also lived there for longer periods.\\nSuch places are called:\\n🏠 Habitation-cum-factory sites\\nThey combined two activities:\\n🏠 Habitation → People lived there\\n🏭 Factory → People made tools there\\n→ 🏠🏭 Habitation-cum-factory\\n⏳ How Do Archaeologists Divide Prehistory?\\nArchaeologists have given names to different periods of early human history.\\nThe earliest period discussed here is called:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\nMeaning: Old Stone\\nThe Palaeolithic period extended from about:\\n2 million years ago → 12,000 years ago\\nIt is divided into:\\n🔹 Lower Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Middle Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Upper Palaeolithic\\nThe name highlights the importance of stone tools found from this period.\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted approximately:\\n12,000 years ago → 10,000 years ago\\nThe stone tools from this period were generally very small.\\nThese small tools are called:\\n🔹 Microliths\\nMicroliths were probably attached to handles made of:\\n🦴 Bone\\n🪵 Wood\\nThey could be used to make tools such as:\\n🪚 Saws\\n🌾 Sickles\\nOlder varieties of tools continued to be used during this period as well.\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago, the next stage is known as the Neolithic.\\nThe Neolithic period will be studied in the next chapter.\\n⏳ PREHISTORIC TIMELINE\\n2 million years ago\\n⬇️\\n🪨 PALAEOLITHIC\\n2 million → 12,000 years ago\\n⬇️\\n🔹 MESOLITHIC\\n12,000 → 10,000 years ago\\n⬇️\\n🌱 NEOLITHIC\\nFrom about 10,000 years ago\\n🗺️ Important Archaeological Sites\\nSome important places mentioned in the chapter include:\\n📍 Bhimbetka — a habitation site with caves and rock shelters in present-day Madhya Pradesh.\\n📍 Hunsgi — an important prehistoric site associated with stone tools.\\n📍 Kurnool Caves — where traces of ash provide evidence of familiarity with fire.\\nThese places help archaeologists understand different aspects of early human life.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🪨 Archaeological sites contain remains left behind by people.\\n🏠 Habitation sites were places where people lived.\\n🏭 Factory sites were places where people made stone tools.\\n🏠🏭 Habitation-cum-factory sites were used for both living and making tools.\\n🪨 Palaeolithic: about 2 million → 12,000 years ago.\\n🔹 Mesolithic: about 12,000 → 10,000 years ago.\\n🔹 Microliths: tiny stone tools of the Mesolithic period.\\n🌱 Neolithic: from about 10,000 years ago.\\n📍 Archaeological sites such as Bhimbetka, Hunsgi and Kurnool provide evidence about early people.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Understanding Sites\\nQuestion:\\nWhat is an archaeological site?\\nA. A modern museum\\nB. A place where remains left behind by people are found\\nC. A place where only animals live\\nD. A modern factory\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nArchaeological sites are places where remains such as tools, pots and buildings made, used and left behind by people are found.\\nMCQ 2 — Prehistoric Timeline\\nQuestion:\\nWhich period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago?\\nA. Palaeolithic\\nB. Mesolithic\\nC. Neolithic\\nD. Modern period\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nThe Mesolithic period lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago. The tiny stone tools found from this period are called microliths.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Discover the Prehistoric Site\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters an unexplored prehistoric landscape.\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“We have discovered a place left behind by early people. Look carefully at the evidence. Can you figure out what happened here and when?”\\nThe player sees several areas:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🏠 Rock shelter\\n🪵 Tool-making area\\n⏳ Timeline markers\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three investigation challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — 🏠 Identify the Site\\nTask:\\nThe player finds a natural rock shelter.\\nWhat was this type of place most likely used for?\\nA. Living\\nB. Making modern machines\\nC. Storing cars\\nD. Building roads\\nPlayer selects:\\n🏠 Living\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 2 — 🏭 Identify the Factory\\nThe player discovers:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔨 Tool-making area\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\nTask:\\nWhat type of site does this evidence suggest?\\nA. Habitation site\\nB. Factory site\\nC. Modern village\\nD. Burial site\\nPlayer selects:\\n🏭 Factory site\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — ⏳ Build the Timeline\\nThe player receives three timeline cards:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\n🌱 Neolithic\\nThe player must arrange them in the correct order.\\nCorrect Sequence:\\n🪨 Palaeolithic\\n↓\\n🔹 Mesolithic\\n↓\\n🌱 Neolithic\\n✅ Correct.\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! You used the evidence to identify the site and then placed the prehistoric periods in the correct order. Archaeologists use remains and dates to build our understanding of the past.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to identify evidence, classify a site and apply the prehistoric timeline, rather than simply memorising definitions.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Site That Tells a Story\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient archaeological site.\\nThere are no people there, but many objects remain.\\nThe player discovers:\\n🪨 Stone blocks\\n🔹 Stone flakes\\n🏠 Rock shelter\\n🔨 Tool-making area\\n⏳ Ancient timeline marker\\nAI Guide:\\n“This place has no written story, but the remains left behind may tell us what happened here. Investigate the evidence.”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🏠 The Rock Shelter\\nThe player examines the shelter.\\nEvidence:\\n“The natural shelter could have protected people from rain, heat and wind.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“This could have been a habitation site where people lived.”\\nClue 2 — 🪨 The Stone Blocks\\nThe player examines large pieces of stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Good-quality stone is available at this location.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Early people looked for places where suitable stone was easily available for making tools.”\\nClue 3 — 🔹 The Stone Flakes\\nThe player discovers many small pieces of broken stone.\\nEvidence:\\n“Chips and waste pieces of stone are scattered around the area.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“These remains can be evidence that stone tools were being made here.”\\nClue 4 — ⏳ The Timeline\\nThe player finds evidence associated with very small stone tools.\\nEvidence:\\n“The tools are tiny and may be microliths.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Microliths were characteristic of the Mesolithic period, which lasted from about 12,000 to 10,000 years ago.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat does the evidence suggest about this place?\\nA. It was a modern industrial factory.\\nB. It was a prehistoric place where people lived and made stone tools.\\nC. It was a modern city.\\nD. It was a railway station.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The shelter suggests that people lived here, while the stone blocks and flakes suggest that tools were made here. The evidence helps archaeologists reconstruct how people lived in the distant past.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:57', '2026-09-10 04:34:57'),
(13, 5, 1, 1, 'Puzzle — 🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence', 'Puzzle game for Stage 5: 🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence', 'Play the Puzzle activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 75, 15, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 5, \"title\": \"🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🎨 What Did Early People Paint?\\nEarly people painted on the walls of caves and rock shelters.\\nMany prehistoric paintings show animals.\\nExamples of animals shown in ancient cave paintings include:\\n🐎 Wild horses\\n🐂 Aurochs — an older, wild form of cattle\\n🦬 Bison\\n🦏 Woolly rhinoceros\\n🦌 Reindeer\\n🐻 Bear\\nSome paintings were made in bright colours.\\n🪨 Where Did the Colours Come From?\\nThe colours used in prehistoric paintings came from natural materials.\\n🎨 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n🔥 Charcoal\\nThese natural materials were used to create different colours for the paintings.\\n🎨 Natural Materials\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n↓\\n🖌️ Paint\\n↓\\n🪨 Cave / Rock Shelter Wall\\n🕵️ Why Did Early People Make Rock Paintings?\\nThe exact reason for making prehistoric paintings is not known for certain.\\nArchaeologists suggest some possible explanations.\\n🎭 Ceremonial occasions\\nThe paintings may have been connected with ceremonies.\\n🔮 Special rituals\\nThey may have been connected with rituals performed by hunters before searching for prey.\\nThe textbook presents these as possibilities, not confirmed facts.\\nSo we should remember:\\n❓ Exact purpose = uncertain\\n💭 Possible reasons = ceremonies or special rituals\\n🏠 Why Were Caves and Rock Shelters Important?\\nCaves and rock shelters were not only places where paintings could be made.\\nThey could also serve as habitation sites, where early people lived.\\nNatural caves provided protection from:\\n🌧️ Rain\\n☀️ Heat\\n💨 Wind\\nThis is why caves and rock shelters were useful places for early people.\\n🔎 How Do Paintings Help Us Understand the Past?\\nArchaeologists study the remains and evidence left behind by people.\\nRock paintings can provide clues about:\\n🐾 Animals known to people\\n🎨 Materials used for painting\\n🏕️ Places where people lived\\n🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Possible activities or rituals\\nBut archaeologists must be careful when interpreting evidence.\\nA painting can show us what was represented, but its exact meaning may not always be known.\\nFor example, the NCERT textbook says the purpose of some paintings is uncertain and gives possible explanations rather than one definite answer.\\n🇮🇳 Rock Paintings in the Past\\nPrehistoric rock paintings are an important part of the evidence used to understand early people.\\nOne well-known example is Bhimbetka, where natural caves and rock shelters provided places where people lived.\\nThe chapter also discusses cave paintings from France, which were made between about 20,000 and 10,000 years ago and included many animal paintings.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🎨 Early people painted on cave and rock shelter walls.\\n🐎 Paintings included animals such as wild horses, bison, reindeer and bears.\\n🟤 Colours could be made from ochre and iron ore.\\n⚫ Charcoal was also used.\\n🎭 Paintings may have been connected with ceremonies or special rituals, but their exact purpose is uncertain.\\n🏠 Caves and rock shelters could also provide shelter from rain, heat and wind.\\n🔎 Archaeologists study paintings and other remains as evidence about the past.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Cave Paintings\\nQuestion:\\nWhere did early people make many prehistoric paintings?\\nA. On modern buildings\\nB. On cave and rock shelter walls\\nC. On paper books\\nD. On metal plates\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nPrehistoric paintings were made on the walls of caves and rock shelters. Many paintings showed animals such as wild horses, bison and bears.\\nMCQ 2 — Natural Colours\\nQuestion:\\nWhich of the following was used to make colours for prehistoric paintings?\\nA. Plastic\\nB. Charcoal\\nC. Glass\\nD. Cement\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nPrehistoric colours could be made from natural materials such as ochre, iron ore and charcoal.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Recreate the Cave Painting\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric rock shelter.\\nOn the ground are several natural materials and objects.\\nAvailable items:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n🎨 Paint mixture\\n🪨 Stone wall\\n🧴 Modern paint\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Look at the materials around you. Early people created paintings using what nature provided. Can you recreate their painting?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — 🎨 Choose Natural Materials\\nTask:\\nSelect the materials that could have been used to make prehistoric colours.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n✅ Correct.\\n❌ Modern paint\\nChallenge 2 — 🪨 Choose the Painting Surface\\nTask:\\nWhere should the player create the prehistoric painting?\\nA. Stone cave wall\\nB. Plastic board\\nC. Glass window\\nD. Metal screen\\nPlayer selects:\\n🪨 Stone cave wall\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — 🐎 Paint the Evidence\\nThe player is shown several images:\\n🐎 Wild horse\\n🦬 Bison\\n🐻 Bear\\n🚗 Modern car\\n🏢 Modern building\\nTask:\\nSelect animals that could appear in prehistoric cave paintings.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🐎 Wild horse\\n🦬 Bison\\n🐻 Bear\\n✅ Correct.\\n❌ Modern car\\n❌ Modern building\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! You used natural materials and selected animals found in prehistoric paintings. These paintings give us clues about the world of early people.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to identify materials, recognise the appropriate painting surface and interpret prehistoric visual evidence.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Story on the Cave Wall\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient rock shelter.\\nOn the wall is a colourful prehistoric painting.\\nNearby, the player discovers:\\n🪨 A rock shelter\\n🎨 A painted wall\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n🐎 Animal figures\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Someone left this painting behind long ago. What can these clues tell us about the people who made it?”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🪨 The Rock Shelter\\nThe player examines the location.\\nEvidence:\\n“The painting is inside a natural rock shelter.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Natural caves and rock shelters could be places where early people lived and found protection from rain, heat and wind.”\\nClue 2 — 🎨 The Painted Wall\\nThe player examines the painting.\\nEvidence:\\n“The wall contains several animal figures.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Animals were commonly represented in prehistoric cave paintings.”\\nClue 3 — 🟤 The Colour Materials\\nThe player discovers natural materials near the painting.\\nEvidence:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\nAI Guide:\\n“These natural materials could have been used to create the colours in prehistoric paintings.”\\nClue 4 — ❓ The Meaning\\nThe player examines the animals and painting style.\\nEvidence:\\n“The exact reason for the painting cannot be determined from the painting alone.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“The paintings may have been connected with ceremonies or special rituals, but their exact purpose is uncertain.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat can we reasonably conclude from the evidence?\\nA. The painting was definitely made for one specific ceremony.\\nB. The painting is evidence that prehistoric people created art, but its exact purpose is uncertain.\\nC. The painting was made using modern paint.\\nD. The painting was created recently.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The cave painting is evidence left behind by prehistoric people. We can identify the animals and natural materials used, but we cannot be certain about the exact purpose of the painting.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:57', '2026-09-10 04:34:57');
