(14, 5, 2, 2, 'Mystery — 🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence', 'Mystery game for Stage 5: 🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence', 'Play the Mystery activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 100, 20, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 5, \"title\": \"🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🎨 What Did Early People Paint?\\nEarly people painted on the walls of caves and rock shelters.\\nMany prehistoric paintings show animals.\\nExamples of animals shown in ancient cave paintings include:\\n🐎 Wild horses\\n🐂 Aurochs — an older, wild form of cattle\\n🦬 Bison\\n🦏 Woolly rhinoceros\\n🦌 Reindeer\\n🐻 Bear\\nSome paintings were made in bright colours.\\n🪨 Where Did the Colours Come From?\\nThe colours used in prehistoric paintings came from natural materials.\\n🎨 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n🔥 Charcoal\\nThese natural materials were used to create different colours for the paintings.\\n🎨 Natural Materials\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n↓\\n🖌️ Paint\\n↓\\n🪨 Cave / Rock Shelter Wall\\n🕵️ Why Did Early People Make Rock Paintings?\\nThe exact reason for making prehistoric paintings is not known for certain.\\nArchaeologists suggest some possible explanations.\\n🎭 Ceremonial occasions\\nThe paintings may have been connected with ceremonies.\\n🔮 Special rituals\\nThey may have been connected with rituals performed by hunters before searching for prey.\\nThe textbook presents these as possibilities, not confirmed facts.\\nSo we should remember:\\n❓ Exact purpose = uncertain\\n💭 Possible reasons = ceremonies or special rituals\\n🏠 Why Were Caves and Rock Shelters Important?\\nCaves and rock shelters were not only places where paintings could be made.\\nThey could also serve as habitation sites, where early people lived.\\nNatural caves provided protection from:\\n🌧️ Rain\\n☀️ Heat\\n💨 Wind\\nThis is why caves and rock shelters were useful places for early people.\\n🔎 How Do Paintings Help Us Understand the Past?\\nArchaeologists study the remains and evidence left behind by people.\\nRock paintings can provide clues about:\\n🐾 Animals known to people\\n🎨 Materials used for painting\\n🏕️ Places where people lived\\n🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Possible activities or rituals\\nBut archaeologists must be careful when interpreting evidence.\\nA painting can show us what was represented, but its exact meaning may not always be known.\\nFor example, the NCERT textbook says the purpose of some paintings is uncertain and gives possible explanations rather than one definite answer.\\n🇮🇳 Rock Paintings in the Past\\nPrehistoric rock paintings are an important part of the evidence used to understand early people.\\nOne well-known example is Bhimbetka, where natural caves and rock shelters provided places where people lived.\\nThe chapter also discusses cave paintings from France, which were made between about 20,000 and 10,000 years ago and included many animal paintings.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🎨 Early people painted on cave and rock shelter walls.\\n🐎 Paintings included animals such as wild horses, bison, reindeer and bears.\\n🟤 Colours could be made from ochre and iron ore.\\n⚫ Charcoal was also used.\\n🎭 Paintings may have been connected with ceremonies or special rituals, but their exact purpose is uncertain.\\n🏠 Caves and rock shelters could also provide shelter from rain, heat and wind.\\n🔎 Archaeologists study paintings and other remains as evidence about the past.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Cave Paintings\\nQuestion:\\nWhere did early people make many prehistoric paintings?\\nA. On modern buildings\\nB. On cave and rock shelter walls\\nC. On paper books\\nD. On metal plates\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nPrehistoric paintings were made on the walls of caves and rock shelters. Many paintings showed animals such as wild horses, bison and bears.\\nMCQ 2 — Natural Colours\\nQuestion:\\nWhich of the following was used to make colours for prehistoric paintings?\\nA. Plastic\\nB. Charcoal\\nC. Glass\\nD. Cement\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nPrehistoric colours could be made from natural materials such as ochre, iron ore and charcoal.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Recreate the Cave Painting\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric rock shelter.\\nOn the ground are several natural materials and objects.\\nAvailable items:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n🎨 Paint mixture\\n🪨 Stone wall\\n🧴 Modern paint\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Look at the materials around you. Early people created paintings using what nature provided. Can you recreate their painting?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — 🎨 Choose Natural Materials\\nTask:\\nSelect the materials that could have been used to make prehistoric colours.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n✅ Correct.\\n❌ Modern paint\\nChallenge 2 — 🪨 Choose the Painting Surface\\nTask:\\nWhere should the player create the prehistoric painting?\\nA. Stone cave wall\\nB. Plastic board\\nC. Glass window\\nD. Metal screen\\nPlayer selects:\\n🪨 Stone cave wall\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — 🐎 Paint the Evidence\\nThe player is shown several images:\\n🐎 Wild horse\\n🦬 Bison\\n🐻 Bear\\n🚗 Modern car\\n🏢 Modern building\\nTask:\\nSelect animals that could appear in prehistoric cave paintings.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🐎 Wild horse\\n🦬 Bison\\n🐻 Bear\\n✅ Correct.\\n❌ Modern car\\n❌ Modern building\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! You used natural materials and selected animals found in prehistoric paintings. These paintings give us clues about the world of early people.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to identify materials, recognise the appropriate painting surface and interpret prehistoric visual evidence.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Story on the Cave Wall\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient rock shelter.\\nOn the wall is a colourful prehistoric painting.\\nNearby, the player discovers:\\n🪨 A rock shelter\\n🎨 A painted wall\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n🐎 Animal figures\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Someone left this painting behind long ago. What can these clues tell us about the people who made it?”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🪨 The Rock Shelter\\nThe player examines the location.\\nEvidence:\\n“The painting is inside a natural rock shelter.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Natural caves and rock shelters could be places where early people lived and found protection from rain, heat and wind.”\\nClue 2 — 🎨 The Painted Wall\\nThe player examines the painting.\\nEvidence:\\n“The wall contains several animal figures.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Animals were commonly represented in prehistoric cave paintings.”\\nClue 3 — 🟤 The Colour Materials\\nThe player discovers natural materials near the painting.\\nEvidence:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\nAI Guide:\\n“These natural materials could have been used to create the colours in prehistoric paintings.”\\nClue 4 — ❓ The Meaning\\nThe player examines the animals and painting style.\\nEvidence:\\n“The exact reason for the painting cannot be determined from the painting alone.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“The paintings may have been connected with ceremonies or special rituals, but their exact purpose is uncertain.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat can we reasonably conclude from the evidence?\\nA. The painting was definitely made for one specific ceremony.\\nB. The painting is evidence that prehistoric people created art, but its exact purpose is uncertain.\\nC. The painting was made using modern paint.\\nD. The painting was created recently.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The cave painting is evidence left behind by prehistoric people. We can identify the animals and natural materials used, but we cannot be certain about the exact purpose of the painting.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:57', '2026-09-10 04:34:57'),
(15, 5, 3, 3, 'Theoretical — 🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence', 'Theoretical game for Stage 5: 🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence', 'Play the Theoretical activity using the authored Chapter 1 content.', 50, 10, '{\"source\": \"History Chapter 1.docx\", \"stage_number\": 5, \"title\": \"🎨 Rock Paintings & Prehistoric Evidence\", \"content\": \"📖 INFORMATION\\n🎨 What Did Early People Paint?\\nEarly people painted on the walls of caves and rock shelters.\\nMany prehistoric paintings show animals.\\nExamples of animals shown in ancient cave paintings include:\\n🐎 Wild horses\\n🐂 Aurochs — an older, wild form of cattle\\n🦬 Bison\\n🦏 Woolly rhinoceros\\n🦌 Reindeer\\n🐻 Bear\\nSome paintings were made in bright colours.\\n🪨 Where Did the Colours Come From?\\nThe colours used in prehistoric paintings came from natural materials.\\n🎨 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n🔥 Charcoal\\nThese natural materials were used to create different colours for the paintings.\\n🎨 Natural Materials\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n↓\\n🖌️ Paint\\n↓\\n🪨 Cave / Rock Shelter Wall\\n🕵️ Why Did Early People Make Rock Paintings?\\nThe exact reason for making prehistoric paintings is not known for certain.\\nArchaeologists suggest some possible explanations.\\n🎭 Ceremonial occasions\\nThe paintings may have been connected with ceremonies.\\n🔮 Special rituals\\nThey may have been connected with rituals performed by hunters before searching for prey.\\nThe textbook presents these as possibilities, not confirmed facts.\\nSo we should remember:\\n❓ Exact purpose = uncertain\\n💭 Possible reasons = ceremonies or special rituals\\n🏠 Why Were Caves and Rock Shelters Important?\\nCaves and rock shelters were not only places where paintings could be made.\\nThey could also serve as habitation sites, where early people lived.\\nNatural caves provided protection from:\\n🌧️ Rain\\n☀️ Heat\\n💨 Wind\\nThis is why caves and rock shelters were useful places for early people.\\n🔎 How Do Paintings Help Us Understand the Past?\\nArchaeologists study the remains and evidence left behind by people.\\nRock paintings can provide clues about:\\n🐾 Animals known to people\\n🎨 Materials used for painting\\n🏕️ Places where people lived\\n🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Possible activities or rituals\\nBut archaeologists must be careful when interpreting evidence.\\nA painting can show us what was represented, but its exact meaning may not always be known.\\nFor example, the NCERT textbook says the purpose of some paintings is uncertain and gives possible explanations rather than one definite answer.\\n🇮🇳 Rock Paintings in the Past\\nPrehistoric rock paintings are an important part of the evidence used to understand early people.\\nOne well-known example is Bhimbetka, where natural caves and rock shelters provided places where people lived.\\nThe chapter also discusses cave paintings from France, which were made between about 20,000 and 10,000 years ago and included many animal paintings.\\n🧠 INFORMATION RECAP\\n🎨 Early people painted on cave and rock shelter walls.\\n🐎 Paintings included animals such as wild horses, bison, reindeer and bears.\\n🟤 Colours could be made from ochre and iron ore.\\n⚫ Charcoal was also used.\\n🎭 Paintings may have been connected with ceremonies or special rituals, but their exact purpose is uncertain.\\n🏠 Caves and rock shelters could also provide shelter from rain, heat and wind.\\n🔎 Archaeologists study paintings and other remains as evidence about the past.\\n🎮 Game\\n🧠 THEORETICAL MODE — 2 MCQs\\nMCQ 1 — Cave Paintings\\nQuestion:\\nWhere did early people make many prehistoric paintings?\\nA. On modern buildings\\nB. On cave and rock shelter walls\\nC. On paper books\\nD. On metal plates\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nPrehistoric paintings were made on the walls of caves and rock shelters. Many paintings showed animals such as wild horses, bison and bears.\\nMCQ 2 — Natural Colours\\nQuestion:\\nWhich of the following was used to make colours for prehistoric paintings?\\nA. Plastic\\nB. Charcoal\\nC. Glass\\nD. Cement\\n✅ Correct Answer: B\\nExplanation:\\nPrehistoric colours could be made from natural materials such as ochre, iron ore and charcoal.\\n🧩 PUZZLE MODE — 1 Puzzle\\nPuzzle: Recreate the Cave Painting\\n🎯 Situation\\nThe player enters a prehistoric rock shelter.\\nOn the ground are several natural materials and objects.\\nAvailable items:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n🎨 Paint mixture\\n🪨 Stone wall\\n🧴 Modern paint\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Look at the materials around you. Early people created paintings using what nature provided. Can you recreate their painting?”\\n🎮 Player Task\\nThe player must complete three challenges.\\nChallenge 1 — 🎨 Choose Natural Materials\\nTask:\\nSelect the materials that could have been used to make prehistoric colours.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n✅ Correct.\\n❌ Modern paint\\nChallenge 2 — 🪨 Choose the Painting Surface\\nTask:\\nWhere should the player create the prehistoric painting?\\nA. Stone cave wall\\nB. Plastic board\\nC. Glass window\\nD. Metal screen\\nPlayer selects:\\n🪨 Stone cave wall\\n✅ Correct.\\nChallenge 3 — 🐎 Paint the Evidence\\nThe player is shown several images:\\n🐎 Wild horse\\n🦬 Bison\\n🐻 Bear\\n🚗 Modern car\\n🏢 Modern building\\nTask:\\nSelect animals that could appear in prehistoric cave paintings.\\nPlayer selects:\\n🐎 Wild horse\\n🦬 Bison\\n🐻 Bear\\n✅ Correct.\\n❌ Modern car\\n❌ Modern building\\n🤖 AI Guide Feedback\\n“Excellent! You used natural materials and selected animals found in prehistoric paintings. These paintings give us clues about the world of early people.”\\nThe puzzle requires the player to identify materials, recognise the appropriate painting surface and interpret prehistoric visual evidence.\\n🔍 MYSTERY MODE — 1 Mystery\\nMystery: The Story on the Cave Wall\\n🎬 Opening\\nThe player enters an ancient rock shelter.\\nOn the wall is a colourful prehistoric painting.\\nNearby, the player discovers:\\n🪨 A rock shelter\\n🎨 A painted wall\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⚫ Charcoal\\n🐎 Animal figures\\nThe AI Guide says:\\n“Someone left this painting behind long ago. What can these clues tell us about the people who made it?”\\n🔎 Investigation\\nClue 1 — 🪨 The Rock Shelter\\nThe player examines the location.\\nEvidence:\\n“The painting is inside a natural rock shelter.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Natural caves and rock shelters could be places where early people lived and found protection from rain, heat and wind.”\\nClue 2 — 🎨 The Painted Wall\\nThe player examines the painting.\\nEvidence:\\n“The wall contains several animal figures.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“Animals were commonly represented in prehistoric cave paintings.”\\nClue 3 — 🟤 The Colour Materials\\nThe player discovers natural materials near the painting.\\nEvidence:\\n🟤 Ochre\\n⛏️ Iron ore\\n⚫ Charcoal\\nAI Guide:\\n“These natural materials could have been used to create the colours in prehistoric paintings.”\\nClue 4 — ❓ The Meaning\\nThe player examines the animals and painting style.\\nEvidence:\\n“The exact reason for the painting cannot be determined from the painting alone.”\\nAI Guide:\\n“The paintings may have been connected with ceremonies or special rituals, but their exact purpose is uncertain.”\\n🕵️ Final Deduction\\nThe player reaches the Conclusion Board:\\nWhat can we reasonably conclude from the evidence?\\nA. The painting was definitely made for one specific ceremony.\\nB. The painting is evidence that prehistoric people created art, but its exact purpose is uncertain.\\nC. The painting was made using modern paint.\\nD. The painting was created recently.\\n✅ Correct: B\\n🤖 Final AI Guide\\n“Mystery solved! The cave painting is evidence left behind by prehistoric people. We can identify the animals and natural materials used, but we cannot be certain about the exact purpose of the painting.”\"}', 1, '2026-09-10 04:34:57', '2026-09-10 04:34:57');

